Note that we need to modify the `createXmlElement`, `setPrivateFieldRef`, and other methods to return mock objects instead of creating actual code models. We also need to add annotations to the mock objects using JMockit's annotation classes (`@MethodUnderTest`).

Also, we use `getObjectsFromOutline` method which returns an array of Object instances representing XML object tree.

The code is a good example of how to write unit tests for an object that depends on external resources, like XML files. It showcases the importance of mocking out these resources when writing unit tests.
